<rml>
<head>
	<title>VaCuus M4 JavaScript Demo</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="m4_demo.rcss"/>
	<!-- Captured at load, executed by the script host at OnDocumentReady -- after the body
	     is shown, never from Plugin::OnDocumentLoad (spec 2(f)); src resolves through the
	     ordered DevUI roots at run time. -->
	<script src="m4_hud_logic.js"></script>
</head>

<!-- `data-model="hud"` is resolved EXACTLY ONCE, in Element::SetParent, when this body is
     parented into the context (ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/Element.cpp:2202-2219), which is
     why vacuus.M4Demo calls BindModel BEFORE LoadDocument -- the m3_demo.rml comment carries
     the full argument. The model is the M3 demo's own FVaCuusDemoModel: the gameplay-fed
     fields below keep flowing through data binding UNCHANGED (spec 9), while the killfeed,
     damage numbers, stance and the second bar are DOM the m4_hud_logic.js script drives
     directly. Stance and Killfeed exist in the struct but are deliberately unbound here:
     JS owns those surfaces now, and binding them too would draw two competing truths. -->
<body data-model="hud">
	<div id="m4-title">VaCuus M4 // JAVASCRIPT DEMO</div>

	<!-- 1. GAMEPLAY-FED SCALARS, exactly M3's pipeline: {{Field}} is DataViewText, fed by
	     the reduced C++ driver through UpdateModel every frame. The one non-M3 row is
	     Stance: its span is EMPTY markup that m4_hud_logic.js fills through innerRML and
	     colors through classList on the C++ driver's old 2s beat -- a JS-owned cell inside
	     a data-bound panel, which is the coexistence claim in one row. -->
	<div id="p-scalars" data-class-alert="bAlert">
		<div class="panel-title">1 &#183; GAME-FED SCALARS ({{Tick}} updates published)</div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">CallSign</span><span class="val">{{CallSign}}</span></div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Ammo</span><span class="val">{{Ammo}}</span></div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Stance (JS classList)</span><span class="val" id="stance-val"></span></div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Zone</span><span class="val">{{Zone}}</span></div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Objective</span><span class="val">{{Objective}}</span></div>
	</div>

	<!-- 2. ONE VALUE, TWO BARS. The top bar is M3's data-style-width binding; the bottom
	     bar is m4_hud_logic.js reading the SAME field through
	     vacuus.model('hud').get('Health') in a rAF callback and writing the width through
	     the style proxy. Both read the same UI shadow, so any daylight between the two
	     bars is a bug in one of the two paths -- the panel is its own assertion. -->
	<div id="p-bar">
		<div class="panel-title">2 &#183; HEALTH: BOUND BAR vs JS rAF BAR</div>
		<div id="bar-track">
			<div id="bar-fill" data-style-width="Health + '%'"/>
		</div>
		<div id="js-bar-track">
			<div id="js-bar-fill"/>
		</div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Health</span><span class="val">{{Health}}</span></div>
	</div>

	<!-- 3. A FLATTENED NESTED STRUCT, unchanged from M3: two leaves, one top-level name,
	     still fed by the C++ driver. -->
	<div id="p-nested">
		<div class="panel-title">3 &#183; NESTED STRUCT (game-fed, unchanged)</div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Target.Designation</span><span class="val">{{Target.Designation}}</span></div>
		<div class="row"><span class="key">Target.Distance</span><span class="val">{{Target.Distance}}</span></div>
	</div>

	<!-- 4. THE M3 REFUSAL BUTTON, NOW HEARD. Same control, new contract (spec 3.10): the
	     assignment reaches FVaCuusScalarDefinition::Set, which routes (ViewId, hud, Ammo,
	     value) to the game thread instead of merely refusing. The demo driver's
	     OnModelWrite handler applies it to the live struct and the next UpdateModel echoes
	     it back through data binding -- the accepted-write loop, one write per click. The
	     shadow itself is never written by the UI (I3 stands); `Ammo - 1` rather than M3's
	     `= 0` so EVERY click carries a fresh value and lands (a write equal to the shadow
	     is swallowed by the echo rule, spec 3.10). -->
	<div id="p-write">
		<div class="panel-title">4 &#183; WRITES ARE ROUTED (the accepted-write loop)</div>
		<div id="probe" data-attr-vacuus-ammo="Ammo">ammo mirrored into an attribute</div>
		<button id="write-btn" class="m3-btn" data-event-click="Ammo = Ammo - 1">expend one round (Ammo = Ammo - 1)</button>
		<div class="hint">Click: OnModelWrite fires on the game thread; the driver mutates the live struct; UpdateModel echoes it back.</div>
	</div>

	<!-- 5. THE KILLFEED, NOW PURE DOM. No data-for, no template: #kill-rows starts empty
	     and m4_hud_logic.js appends a .kill-row div per 1.5s beat via createElement +
	     innerRML, trimming above 6 from the front -- the same serial arithmetic as the C++
	     driver (pools 5/7/4, HS on serial%3==0), so a screenshot's rows are computable
	     from elapsed time alone. -->
	<div id="p-kills">
		<div class="panel-title">5 &#183; KILLFEED (JS createElement/remove, <span id="kill-count">0</span> rows)</div>
		<div id="kill-rows"></div>
	</div>

	<!-- 6. DAMAGE NUMBERS: born on a 0.9s interval, each removed by its own 1.2s
	     setTimeout -- spec 9's "damage numbers via timers", value and crit computable
	     from the damage serial. -->
	<div id="p-dmg">
		<div class="panel-title">6 &#183; DAMAGE NUMBERS (JS timers)</div>
		<div id="dmg-zone"></div>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
